  "query_preview": "..A chaotic pattern continues with a number of upper\nlevel lows crossing the eastern Pacific into the West Coast this\nmorning. This will keep the region in an active pattern though at\nleast next weekend. \n\nMorning upper air and satellite analysis indicates an upper level\nlow is approaching the southern Oregon/northern California Coast.\nAn ejecting shortwave trough ahead of this upper level low is\ncrossing Utah this morning. Outside of a few isolated showers,\nthis shortwave trough will mainly bring an area of clouds through\nthe region into early this afternoon. \n\nAhead of the main upper level low and associated cold front, gusty\nsouth to southwesterly winds will continue across the western\nvalleys through midnight tonight, though many sheltered locations\nwill see winds diminish earlier, around 8 PM or so. Wind\nadvisories continue for these locations.\n\nThe aforementioned cold front will approach the Utah/Nevada border\nafter midnight tonight and reach the Wasatch Front around 8-10 AM,\nand Castle Country around 1 PM or so. Forcing for precipitation\nwill be weak with this front and totals will be enhanced by a\nperiod of orographic enhanced west to northwest flow Wednesday\nnight into Thursday. Expect around 0.25\" of rain for the northern\nvalleys and 2 to 6 inches of snow for the northern mountains by\nThursday morning. \n\nAs the cold front crosses Castle Country, ensemble members\ncontinue to indicate a greater than 50% chance of northwest wind \ngusts in excess of 45 mph behind the front along and near the \nSR-10 corridor through about 4-6 PM. Meanwhile, ahead of the \nfront, ensemble members suggest a greater than 50% chance of wind \ngusts up to 50 mph across the eastern valleys including the San \nRafael Swell, the Capitol Reef area, western Canyonlands, the Glen\nCanyon Recreation Area, and portions of the Grand Staircase- \nEscalante Monument after 9 AM Wednesday through about 6 PM. Wind \nadvisories are now in effect for these areas for Wednesday.\n\nIn the wake of this cold front, another round of freeze conditions\nwill impact many Utah valleys. For Wednesday night into Thursday\nnight, there is a greater than 70% chance temperatures will fall \nbelow 32F in the Sanpete and Sevier Valleys, west central and \nsouthwest Utah. Issued a Freeze Watch for these locations. Note \nthat in particular for the Sanpete and Sevier Valleys, there is \naround 50% chance temperatures will fall below 28F, which will \ncause fruit tree blossom death in unmitigated orchards. Freeze\nWatches have been issued for these locations.\n\nAnother round of very cold temperatures will bring freezes to\nadditional valleys beyond the Sanpete, Sevier and west central and\nsouthwest Utah Thursday night into Friday morning including\neastern Box Elder County, the western Uinta Basin and Castle\nCountry. Future shifts will evaluate trends and issue Freeze\nWatches or Warnings if needed.\n\nHigh temperatures will trend around 5 to 10 degrees below normal\nthrough Friday before the next storm approaches the area Saturday.\nWhile the majority of ensemble members bring some type of\nshortwave trough and/or upper level low through the region, the\npath and strength of this feature varies widely across the\nensemble members. Around 20% of the members bring a stronger\ntrough through, with a higher precipitation potential for \nnorthern Utah, whereas 15% bring a stronger upper level low \nthrough southern Utah. Something to monitor moving forward, though\nno particular solution looks particularly impactful outside of \nbringing cooler temperatures and the chance of precipitation for \nSunday.",
